  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nWednesday, a stronger longwave trough will become established across \nthe Hudson Bay region, resulting in northwest flow aloft across the \ncentral Plains. Embedded within this flow, a weak mid-level \ndisturbance is forecast to move from Wyoming into northwest and \nnorth-central Nebraska Wednesday morning. This disturbance will \nresult in increasing mid-level warm air advection, frontogenesis, \nand differential cyclonic vorticity advection (DCVA), providing \nsufficient lift for more widespread shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment through the day Wednesday. Moisture across the region \nwill be quite impressive, with recent model guidance depicting \nprecipitable water (PWAT) values exceeding 1.5 inches. These values \nare near the 95th percentile to near the climatological maximum for \nthis time of year, highlighting just how anomalous the moisture \nprofile will be. Forecast soundings across the area depict deep \nmoisture profiles supportive of efficient rainfall processes and \nlocally heavy rainfall. The NBM currently depicts around a 50 \npercent probability of rainfall exceeding one-half inch along a \nValentine to Broken Bow corridor. There remains considerable \nuncertainty regarding where the heaviest rainfall axis will \nultimately set up, so this will continue to be monitored over the \nnext several forecast cycles. High temperatures Wednesday will range \nfrom the middle 70s across north-central Nebraska, where cloud cover \nis expected to persist through much of the day, to the middle and \nupper 80s across western and southwest Nebraska. \n\nThursday, the longwave trough will remain across the northeastern \nUnited States, maintaining northwest flow aloft over the region as \nanother weak embedded shortwave moves east across the central \nPlains. This will provide another opportunity for showers and \nthunderstorms, though model guidance remains in disagreement \nregarding where the heaviest precipitation will occur. Temperatures \nwill remain below normal, with highs in the upper 70s to middle 80s \nas below-normal 850-mb temperature anomalies and persistent cloud \ncover continue across much of the area.\n\nFriday through the weekend, the upper-level ridge centered across \nthe southern Plains is expected to strengthen and gradually expand \nnorthward into the central Plains. By Saturday, forecast 500-mb \nheights are expected to approach 597 dam. In addition, 850-mb \ntemperature anomalies are forecast to increase to 4 to 8\u00b0C above \nnormal, with the greatest anomalies focused across the Dakotas and \nnorthern Plains. This pattern will favor a return to hot and \npredominantly dry conditions as the primary storm track shifts north \nof the region. High temperatures are expected to climb into the \nmiddle to upper 90s across much of western and north-central \nNebraska by Saturday and Sunday, with some locations across \nnorthern, western, and southwestern Nebraska potentially reaching \nthe lower 100s. Heat headlines may eventually be needed, though \nconfidence in the magnitude and duration of the hottest temperatures \nremains too low to introduce any headlines at this time.",
